## GraphQL API

Single endpoint: `POST /graphql` with a JSON body. There is no Playground or GraphiQL; `GET`, form posts and operations in the URL are refused, so a cross-site link or form can't run a mutation. Introspection is off unless `GRAPHQL_INTROSPECTION=true`, and a document may select at most 10 root fields and 100 fields in total (each root field is a datastore call; the limits are in the handler). Schema and resolvers live in `internal/adapters/handlers/graphql`.

- Type `User { id: String, name: String }`
- Type `UserPage { items: [User], next_cursor: String }`
- Queries: `user(id: String): User` · `users(limit: Int, cursor: String): UserPage`
- Mutations: `create(name: String!): String` (the new id) · `update(id: String!, name: String): User` · `delete(id: String!): Boolean` (a missing id is the error `user not found`)

`users` is cursor-paginated exactly like the REST list (opaque `next_cursor`, default limit `20`, cap `100`); pass a previous page's `next_cursor` as the `cursor` argument to continue.

```bash
# create (server on :8080 by default — see PORT)
{{- if $hasAuth}}
# /graphql is behind the bearer token, so mint one first; without it every call is 401
TOKEN=$(curl -s -XPOST localhost:8080/login -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"username":"admin","password":"<PASSWORD>"}' | jq -r .token)
curl -s -XPOST localhost:8080/graphql -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":"mutation { create(name: \"alice\") }"}'
# query a page
curl -s -XPOST localhost:8080/graphql -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":"{ users(limit: 2) { items { id name } next_cursor } }"}'
{{- else}}
curl -s -XPOST localhost:8080/graphql -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":"mutation { create(name: \"alice\") }"}'
# query a page
curl -s -XPOST localhost:8080/graphql -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":"{ users(limit: 2) { items { id name } next_cursor } }"}'
{{- end}}
```
{{- end}}
{{- if $isWeb}}

## Web UI

Server-rendered HTML (this is **not** a JSON API). Handlers are in `internal/adapters/handlers/{{.Platform}}`; routes live under `/users`. **The pages are read-only**: there are no create, edit or delete forms yet. Writes are form-encoded requests to the same routes, sent with curl or another client:

| Method | Path | Does |
|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/users` | list page (an empty list says how to add a user) |
| `GET` | `/users/:id` | detail page, with a link back to the list |
| `POST` | `/users` | create — form field `name` |
| `PUT` | `/users/:id` | update — form field `name` |
| `DELETE` | `/users/:id` | delete |

A missing user, a bad page link and a server failure render an HTML error page (`internal/web/templates/error.html`, via `web.RenderError`) with the matching status. The write routes answer in plain text for their clients. Adding forms means adding them to the page templates; the cross-origin protection already accepts same-origin form posts.

The list page is **forward-only paginated**: when more rows remain it renders a `Next →` link carrying an opaque `?cursor=…` (default page size `20`). This is a deliberately minimal browse UX — there is no "Previous" (backward cursors are out of scope). A hand-edited, malformed `?cursor` surfaces as a `400`; the generated `Next` links always carry a valid cursor.

Static assets are served from `/assets/*`. HTML templates and CSS live in `internal/web` and are **embedded into the binary** via `//go:embed`, so the compiled binary is self-contained (no runtime file dependencies). The server listens on `:8080` by default (see `PORT`).
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasHTTP}}

## Health & readiness

Two orchestration endpoints are mounted at the transport layer (in `cmd/server`), so they work regardless of the HTTP framework:

| Method | Path | Purpose | Success |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/healthz` | liveness — the process is running | `200 {"status":"ok"}` |
| `GET` | `/readyz` | readiness — ready to accept traffic | `200 {"status":"ready"}` |

Wire `/healthz` to a liveness probe and `/readyz` to a readiness probe (e.g. Kubernetes). The handlers live in `internal/adapters/handlers/health`. {{if $hasRealDB}}`/readyz` pings the `{{.Database}}` repository (2 s budget) and answers `503 {"status":"unavailable","failed":["database"]}` while it is down; the cause goes to the log. {{end}}To probe another dependency, pass one more `health.Check` to `server.Serve` in `cmd/main.go`.{{if $hasRealDB}}

At startup the repository pings `{{.Database}}`{{if not (or (eq .Database "sqlite") (eq .Database "badgerdb"))}} with a 5 s budget{{end}}, so a wrong host, port or password stops the app with an error instead of failing every request.{{end}}
