## API documentation (OpenAPI / Swagger)

The API is documented by an OpenAPI 3.0.3 spec served by the app, with interactive Swagger UI:

| Method | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/openapi.yaml` | the raw OpenAPI document (`application/yaml`) |
| `GET` | `/swagger/` | interactive Swagger UI ("try it out") |

Both are mounted at the transport layer in `cmd/server` (so they work for every framework) and are **self-contained**: the spec is embedded via `//go:embed` and the Swagger UI assets are compiled into the binary by `swaggest/swgui` — no CDN, nothing to deploy alongside. The serving handlers are `api.SpecHandler` / `api.SwaggerUIHandler` in `internal/adapters/api`.{{if .Auth}} The spec declares a `bearerAuth` security scheme; use `POST /login` to get a token, then paste it into Swagger UI's **Authorize** dialog.{{end}}

**The spec is templated, not scraped** — it lives in `internal/adapters/api/openapi.yaml` and was written to match the generated handlers. It is **not** auto-derived from them, so **when you add or change a route, update `openapi.yaml` too** (same discipline as this file and the README). If you'd rather have the spec regenerated from code, swap to annotation-driven generation with [swaggo/swag](https://github.com/swaggo/swag) (`// @Summary`/`// @Router` comments + a `swag init` build step) — more machinery, but it re-derives the doc from annotated handlers.
{{- end}}

## Middleware

Requests to the application handler pass through a framework-agnostic middleware chain composed in `cmd/server` (package `internal/adapters/handlers/middleware`):

- **recover**: a panic becomes a logged `500` with its stack, not a crash. `http.ErrAbortHandler` passes through untouched, and a panic after the response started aborts the connection rather than appending a `500`.
- **request logging**: method, path, status, duration.
- **CORS**: only the origins in `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` (comma-separated `scheme://host[:port]`) get CORS headers; empty means same-origin only. Never use `*`.
- **cross-origin protection**: `http.CrossOriginProtection` answers `403` to a state-changing request a browser sent from another origin (the CORS allowlist is trusted). Non-browser clients such as curl are unaffected.
- **body cap**: request bodies over 1 MiB (`middleware.MaxBodyBytes`) get `413`.{{if eq .ProjectType "api"}}
- **JSON only**: a `POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH` without `Content-Type: application/json` gets `415`, so a cross-site form or `text/plain` fetch can't reach a handler.{{end}}
- **per-request timeout**: bounds the request `context` so a slow datastore call can't hang forever.

**Security headers** (`nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, a strict `Content-Security-Policy`{{if .HasOpenAPI}}, skipped under `/swagger/` because Swagger UI runs inline scripts{{end}}) wrap the whole server, health endpoints included. The health endpoints are mounted outside the chain, so probes stay cheap and unlogged.
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasObservability}}

## Observability (OpenTelemetry)

This project exports **traces and metrics** over OTLP/gRPC to a collector. The setup lives in `internal/adapters/observability` — the only package that imports the OTel SDK. The core stays free of it (a span rides inside `context.Context`), and `make arch` enforces that.

- **Instrumented:** {{if $hasHTTP}}inbound HTTP requests (one span each, via `otelhttp` at the transport wrap in `cmd/server`){{else}}each consumed message (a span in `cmd/main`){{end}}, plus **Go runtime metrics** (GC, goroutines, heap) and W3C trace-context propagation.
- **Config is all `OTEL_*` env vars in `.env`** — endpoint, `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`, resource attributes, and the trace sampler (`OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER`/`_ARG`) are read natively by the SDK; no code override. Lower the sampler ratio in production and/or tail-sample in the collector.
- **Local pipeline:** `make docker-up` starts an `otel-collector` (config `configs/otel-collector.yaml`) that logs received telemetry. Add a real backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Prometheus, a vendor) by extending that config — no app change.
- **Fail-open:** if the collector is unreachable the app still starts and {{if $hasHTTP}}serves{{else}}runs{{end}}; export errors are silenced (`otel.SetErrorHandler`) so they don't flood logs. Buffered telemetry flushes on shutdown{{if $hasHTTP}}, after the HTTP server drains{{else}}, after the consumer goroutine is joined{{end}}.
- **Adding instrumentation:** do it in **adapters** or `cmd`, never the core. Grab a tracer with `otel.Tracer("name")` and start a span from the request/message `context.Context`. A business-logic span belongs behind a port, not a direct SDK import in `services`.
{{- if $hasHTTP}}
- **Route labels:** {{if eq .Platform "graphql"}}every request is one `/graphql` span, so per-operation timing needs resolver-level spans.{{else}}span names follow the route template (`GET {{if $isWeb}}/users{{else}}/api/v1/users{{end}}/{id}`; see `spanName` in `cmd/server`, and keep it in step with the routes).{{end}} Metrics aren't split per route: the HTTP wrap is framework-agnostic and doesn't see the framework's routes, so for per-endpoint RED metrics add framework middleware (`otelecho`/`otelgin`/…).
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasAuth}}

## Authentication (JWT)

Every application route requires a bearer token; a demo `POST /login` issues one. The logic lives in `internal/adapters/handlers/auth` (the only package that imports the JWT library — the core stays free of it; the authenticated subject travels in `context.Context`, readable via `auth.SubjectFrom(ctx)`).

- **Flow:** `POST /login` with `{"username","password"}` returns `{"token":"…"}`; send it as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` (scheme case-insensitive) on every other route. Missing/invalid/expired → `401` with a `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer` challenge.
- **Config** is `JWT_*`/`AUTH_*` in `.env`, read and validated by `auth.LoadConfig` in `cmd/server`. The server **refuses to start** when `JWT_SECRET` is missing, shorter than 32 bytes or the old placeholder, when `JWT_ISSUER`, `JWT_AUDIENCE` or `AUTH_USERNAME` is missing, when `AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH` is missing, malformed or the hash of the old demo password, or when the retired plaintext `AUTH_PASSWORD` is still set. gopamin wrote a random secret and password hash at generation and printed the password once; `make hash-password` (`cmd/hashpassword`) prints the hash for a new one.
- **Tokens:** **HS256**, algorithm **pinned** (`WithValidMethods`) so `alg:none` and RS256→HS256 confusion are rejected; `iss` and `aud` are issued and checked (`JWT_ISSUER`, `JWT_AUDIENCE`), so another service that shares the secret but not both names can't mint accepted tokens.
- **Password:** `AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH` is PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 600,000 iterations (standard library `crypto/pbkdf2`). The password is checked even for an unknown username, so timing doesn't reveal which usernames exist.
- **Login throttling:** `/login` allows 5 attempts per username and 20 per client address in each one-minute window, then answers `429` with `Retry-After`; a success clears the username's counter. Every attempt is logged as `auth.login.success`, `auth.login.failure` or `auth.login.throttled`, with the client address and username. Limits of this in-memory limiter: it is **per instance** (N replicas allow N times the attempts); behind a reverse proxy every client shares the proxy's address, so derive the client from the proxy's forwarding header only once you trust that proxy; anyone can **lock out a known username** for a minute by spending its 5 attempts; it tracks at most 10,000 keys and, once full of live windows, refuses new keys until one expires. Put a shared limiter (gateway or Redis) in front for production.
- **All-or-nothing:** the auth middleware wraps the whole framework handler, so **every** app route is protected; the only public endpoints are `/healthz`, `/readyz`{{if .OpenAPI}}, `/openapi.yaml`, `/swagger/`{{end}} and `/login`. For mixed public/protected app routes, add per-framework middleware (echo groups, chi sub-routers, …).
{{- if $isGraphql}}
- **GraphQL note:** `/graphql` is fully behind the bearer token — send it in `Authorization`. Introspection, when enabled, requires a token too; expose public queries via per-framework middleware if you need them.
{{- end}}
- **Productionize before shipping:** load `JWT_SECRET` from a secret store and rotate it; replace the single env-configured user in `LoginHandler` with a **real user store**. Serve over TLS only (a bearer token is a password). Not yet included: refresh tokens, revocation, MFA, and role/scope authorization.
{{- end}}
{{- if $hasBroker}}

## Broker workflow
{{- if $isWorker}}

This worker has **no HTTP server**. `cmd/main.go`:

1. connects to **{{.Broker}}** (adapter: `internal/adapters/brokers/{{.Broker}}`){{if $hasRealDB}} and `{{.Database}}`{{end}};
2. publishes one sample `user.created` event, for a user named `Hello World`, to the `{{.Slug}}-users` {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}topic{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}queue{{else}}channel{{end}} (`{{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}KAFKA_TOPIC{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}RABBITMQ_QUEUE{{else}}REDIS_CHANNEL{{end}}`);
3. consumes until `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM`: {{if $hasRealDB}}each `user.created` event becomes a user, at most once per event id (`CreateOnce`), so a redelivered event doesn't add a second user; other event types are logged and skipped{{else}}each event is logged as `event received` with its type, id and size{{end}}.
{{- else}}

This {{if $isWeb}}web app{{else}}API{{end}} adds event messaging **alongside** its HTTP server (one binary):

1. **Producer** — after a create, update or delete commits, the user service publishes `user.created`, `user.updated` or `user.deleted` through its `EventPublisher` port (wired with `WithPublisher`). The events adapter (`internal/adapters/events`) encodes it; the broker adapter (`internal/adapters/brokers/{{.Broker}}`) sends it to the `{{.Slug}}-users` {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}topic{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}queue{{else}}channel{{end}} (`{{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}KAFKA_TOPIC{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}RABBITMQ_QUEUE{{else}}REDIS_CHANNEL{{end}}`). The core depends on the port, not on either adapter.
2. **Consumer** — a consume loop runs next to the HTTP server and logs each event as `event received` with its type, id and size, never the payload (it holds a user's name). If the consumer fails, the server drains and the process exits 1, so its supervisor restarts it.
3. `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` drains the HTTP server and stops the consumer together.
{{- end}}

**Event format.** Every message is a versioned JSON envelope owned by `internal/adapters/events`; `id` is unique per event, so a consumer can tell a redelivery from a new event. A message that isn't such an envelope is logged and dropped.

```json
{"id":"<uuid>","type":"user.created","version":1,"occurred_at":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z","data":{"id":"<user id>","name":"Ada"}}
```

**Delivery.** {{if eq .Broker "redis"}}Redis pub/sub is **at-most-once**: a message published while this process is down or reconnecting is lost, and every replica receives every message. Use Kafka or RabbitMQ when a message must not be lost.{{else}}**At-least-once**: {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}the consumer group (`KAFKA_GROUP_ID`) commits a message's offset{{else}}the consumer acknowledges a message{{end}} only after the handler returns nil, so a message whose processing didn't finish (a crash, or a shutdown during a retry) is delivered again{{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}. Replicas in one group split the partitions{{end}}. Keep handlers idempotent{{if and $isWorker $hasRealDB}}, as `CreateOnce` is{{end}}.{{end}} A failing handler is retried with backoff (0.5 s, doubling to 30 s); each attempt has 30 s and isn't cancelled by the shutdown signal, so a write is never cut off halfway. There is no dead-letter {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}topic{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}queue{{else}}channel{{end}}: a handler returns nil for a message it can never process{{if and $isWorker $hasRealDB}}, as the worker does for malformed and invalid events{{end}}.

{{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}**Publishing** waits for every in-sync replica (`RequireAll`) and gives up after 2 s. A publish that timed out can still be delivered, so {{if $isWorker}}a failed publish means "maybe sent"{{else}}`500 Event publish failure` means "maybe sent"{{end}}.{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}**Publishing** sends persistent messages to a durable queue and waits for the broker's confirm (2 s budget). A queue that already exists as non-durable can't be redeclared durable (`PRECONDITION_FAILED`, 406): delete it once, or set another `RABBITMQ_QUEUE`.{{else}}**Publishing** reaches only the subscribers connected at that moment. Set `REDIS_TLS=true` for a server that requires TLS.{{end}}
{{- end}}

