## Database migrations

{{if eq .Database "sqlite"}}The SQLite schema is owned by versioned migration files in `migrations/` ([golang-migrate](https://github.com/golang-migrate/migrate)). The SQLite adapter applies them at startup from a copy embedded in the binary (`migrations/migrations.go`), so a container from the image has its schema; `make migrate-up` writes the same version table, so running both is harmless. Only SQLite does this: a database server may have several instances starting at once, which would race on DDL.{{else}}The `{{.Database}}` schema is owned by versioned migration files in `migrations/` (applied with [golang-migrate](https://github.com/golang-migrate/migrate)), **not** created by the app at startup — so multiple instances never race on DDL, and schema changes are deliberate and reviewable.{{end}}

```bash
make migrate-up                        # apply all pending migrations{{if ne .Database "sqlite"}} (run before first boot){{end}}
make migrate-down                      # roll back the most recent migration
make migrate-create name=add_email     # scaffold a new up/down pair under migrations/
```

The migrate CLI is pinned and run via `go run` (never added to `go.mod`), mirroring `make lint`/`make arch`. When you change the schema, add a migration pair rather than editing the repository constructor — the adapter assumes the table already exists.
{{end}}{{- if ne .ProjectType "hello-world"}}

## Container image

The `Dockerfile` builds the binary into a non-root distroless image{{if eq .Database "sqlite"}} (with libc, because the SQLite driver uses cgo){{end}}. The image holds no `.env`, so pass the configuration when you run it:

```bash
docker build -t {{.Slug}} .
docker run --rm --env-file .env{{if $hasHTTP}} -p 8080:8080{{end}} {{.Slug}}
```
{{- if or .HasContainerizedBackend .Observability}}

Inside a container, `localhost` is the container itself, so the hosts in `.env`, written for `make run`, don't reach the compose services. Add `--network <directory>_default` (the network `docker compose` created; `docker network ls` lists it) and override the hosts with `-e`. On that network each service listens on its own default port, so if you changed a `*_PORT` in `.env`, override it back too (for example `-e POSTGRES_PORT=5432`).

| Variable | Value on the compose network |
|---|---|
{{if eq .Database "postgres"}}| `POSTGRES_HOST` | `psql` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Database "mysql"}}| `MYSQL_HOST` | `mysql` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Database "mariadb"}}| `MARIADB_HOST` | `mariadb` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Database "mongodb"}}| `MONGODB_URI` | the same URI with the host `mongo:27017` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Database "cassandra"}}| `CASSANDRA_HOST` | `cassandra` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Database "dynamodb"}}| `DYNAMODB_ENDPOINT` | `http://dynamodb:8000` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}| `RABBITMQ_HOST` | `rabbitmq` |
{{end}}{{if eq .Broker "redis"}}| `REDIS_HOST` | `redis` |
{{end}}{{if .Observability}}| `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` | `http://otel-collector:4317` |
{{end}}{{- if eq .Broker "kafka"}}
The compose Kafka advertises `localhost:${KAFKA_PORT}`, so a client on the network would be sent back to itself. Point `KAFKA_BROKER` at a broker whose advertised address the container can reach, or use `--network host` on Linux.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if eq .Database "sqlite"}}

The SQLite file lives in the working directory, `/home/nonroot`; mount a volume there (`-v {{.Slug}}-data:/home/nonroot`) to keep it. The app applies the migrations itself at startup.
{{- else if eq .Database "badgerdb"}}

Badger writes to `/home/nonroot/badger`; mount a volume at `/home/nonroot` (`-v {{.Slug}}-data:/home/nonroot`) to keep it.
{{- else if .IsRelational}}

Apply the migrations (`make migrate-up`) before the first start; the app doesn't create its schema.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

## Environment variables

Configuration is loaded from `.env` at the project root (via `godotenv`). Real shell environment variables take precedence over `.env`, so you can override any key inline{{if $hasHTTP}} (e.g. `PORT=9000 make run`){{else}} (e.g. `LOG_LEVEL=debug make run`){{end}}.

{{if $hasHTTP}}- `PORT` — HTTP listen port (default `8080`).
{{end}}{{if $hasRealDB}}{{if eq .Database "sqlite"}}- `SQLITE_DB_URL` — the SQLite database file path (embedded; default `./test.db`, no server).
{{else if eq .Database "badgerdb"}}- BadgerDB is embedded and writes its data under `./badger` — it has no connection settings in `.env`.
{{else if eq .Database "dynamodb"}}- `DYNAMODB_ENDPOINT` (DynamoDB Local; unset for AWS), `DYNAMODB_REGION`, and `DYNAMODB_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`DYNAMODB_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`. Set both keys or neither: with neither, the AWS SDK's default credential chain (profile, SSO, or the ECS/EC2/EKS role) supplies them.
{{else if eq .Database "mongodb"}}- `MONGODB_URI` — the full connection string the app uses (credentials, hosts, replica set, `tls=true`); `MONGODB_NAME` — the database. `MONGODB_USERNAME`/`MONGODB_PASSWORD`/`MONGODB_PORT` configure only the compose container.
{{else if eq .Database "postgres"}}- `POSTGRES_*` connection settings — host, port, database and credentials, plus `POSTGRES_SSLMODE` (required): `disable` only for the local compose database, `verify-full` for a real server.
{{else}}- `{{.Database}}` connection settings — host, port, and credentials. See `.env` for the exact keys.
{{end}}{{end}}{{if $hasBroker}}- {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}`KAFKA_BROKER`, `KAFKA_TOPIC` and `KAFKA_GROUP_ID` (the consumer group){{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}`RABBITMQ_HOST`, `RABBITMQ_PORT`, `RABBITMQ_USER`, `RABBITMQ_PASSWORD` and `RABBITMQ_QUEUE`{{else}}`REDIS_HOST`, `REDIS_PORT`, `REDIS_PASSWORD`, `REDIS_CHANNEL` and `REDIS_TLS`{{end}} — the broker connection and the {{if eq .Broker "kafka"}}topic{{else if eq .Broker "rabbitmq"}}queue{{else}}channel{{end}} (default `{{.Slug}}-users`, so projects sharing a broker don't share it).
{{end}}{{if .Auth}}- `JWT_SECRET`, `JWT_TTL`, `JWT_ISSUER`, `JWT_AUDIENCE`, `AUTH_USERNAME`, `AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH` — JWT signing + demo login (see the Authentication section).
{{end}}{{if .Observability}}- `OTEL_*` — OpenTelemetry exporter endpoint, sampler, and resource attributes (see the Observability section).
{{end}}See `.env` for the full, authoritative list of keys this project reads.

## Testing

```bash
make test          # go test ./...
make test-cover    # run tests and open an HTML coverage report
```
{{if $hasRepo}}The service layer is unit-tested against a fake repository defined in the test itself (`fakeRepository` in `internal/core/user/services`), so tests need no running {{if $hasBroker}}database or broker{{else}}database{{end}} and import no adapter. {{end}}Tests are co-located (`*_test.go`). `make arch` skips test files (`excludeFiles` in `.go-arch-lint.yml`), so a test may wire real adapters; boundaries are enforced on production code.

## Adding a feature

1. **New aggregate** (e.g. `order`): create `internal/core/order/{domain,ports,services}` as a **sibling** of `user/` — a self-contained bounded context. Do **not** grow `user/`. For a **new operation on an existing aggregate**, extend that aggregate's `ports` (the interface) and `services` (the logic).
2. Implement the adapter under the matching `internal/adapters/...` package.
3. Wire the concrete adapter into the composition root in `cmd/main.go`.
4. If you added a new aggregate, mirror the `user-*` block in `.go-arch-lint.yml` (`order-domain` / `order-ports` / `order-services`) so `make arch` keeps enforcing the layering — deny-by-default fails the build on an unattached package until you do.
{{if $isRest}}
To add an endpoint: add the port method and service logic, then a handler in `internal/adapters/handlers/{{.Platform}}` and register its route. Return results through the `api.Response` envelope (errors under `error`, data under `data`).
{{- else if $isGraphql}}
To add an operation: add the field/resolver in `internal/adapters/handlers/graphql` and the backing service method behind a port.
{{- else if $isWeb}}
To add a page: add the service method behind a port, a handler in `internal/adapters/handlers/{{.Platform}}`, and the template/asset under `internal/web`.
{{- else if $isWorker}}
To handle a new message type: add the domain type and a service method, then dispatch to it from the consume loop in `cmd/main.go`.
{{- end}}

## Conventions
- Keep everything under `internal/core/**` free of framework, database, and HTTP imports (`make arch` enforces this).
- Adapters translate between the outside world and domain types; they hold no business rules.
- Co-locate tests (`*_test.go`); the service layer is unit-tested against a fake repository defined in its test file.
- Run `gofmt`, `go vet ./...`, `go fix ./...` (Go 1.27 modernizers; preview with `make fix-check`), `make lint` (golangci-lint), and `make arch` before committing.
